\title{Minimal Obstructions to Total Rainbow Forests:\\ An Edge-Deletion Analysis}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study obstructions to the existence of large \emph{total rainbow forests} in edge-colored graphs, framed through matroid intersection. A total rainbow forest is a set of edges that is simultaneously a forest (independent in the graphic matroid) and rainbow (independent in the color partition matroid); by Edmonds' Matroid Intersection Theorem, the maximum size of such a set equals the minimum over all edge subsets $A$ of the objective $\obj(A) = r_1(A) + r_2(\comp{A})$, where $r_1, r_2$ are the two rank functions and $\comp{A} = E \setminus A$. A graph is an \emph{obstruction} at target $t$ if $\obj(A) < t$ for some $A$. Motivated by the conjecture that a \emph{minimal} obstruction fails the corresponding inequality for exactly one subset, we analyze minimality under edge deletion and prove a sharp negative result: edge-minimal obstructions do not exist for monotone matroid ranks. The mechanism is a monotonicity principle---the Edmonds objective can only decrease under edge deletion---which makes failure of the Rainbow Forest Inequality (RFI) closed under deletion. We prove (i) the weak-duality half of Edmonds' theorem, (ii) deletion monotonicity of the objective, (iii) that a single satisfied deletion forces the inequality for the whole graph, and (iv) the resulting non-existence of edge-minimal obstructions. We confirm non-vacuity with a concrete two-edge obstruction, and contrast the negative deletion result with the positive lattice structure of failing certificates for a fixed obstruction. All results hold for arbitrary monotone integer rank functions on a finite ground set.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Let $G$ be a finite graph whose edge set $E = E(G)$ has been assigned colors. Two independence structures live on the ground set $E$:
\begin{itemize}
\item the \textbf{graphic (cycle) matroid} $M_1$, whose independent sets are the \emph{forests} of $G$, with rank function $r_1$; for $A \subseteq E$, $r_1(A)$ is the maximum size of a forest contained in $A$ (equivalently $r_1(A) = |V| - c(A)$, with $c(A)$ the number of components of $(V,A)$);
\item the \textbf{partition matroid} $M_2$ induced by the coloring, whose independent sets are the \emph{rainbow} edge sets (at most one edge per color), with rank function $r_2$; for $A \subseteq E$, $r_2(A)$ is the number of distinct colors among the edges of $A$.
\end{itemize}

A \textbf{total rainbow forest} is a common independent set of $M_1$ and $M_2$: a set of edges that is both a forest and rainbow. Its maximum size is governed by \textbf{Edmonds' Matroid Intersection Theorem}. Writing $\comp{A} = E \setminus A$ and defining the \textbf{Edmonds intersection objective}
\[
\obj(A) = r_1(A) + r_2(\comp{A}),
\]
Edmonds' theorem states
\[
\max\{ |I| : I \text{ a total rainbow forest} \} = \min_{A \subseteq E} \obj(A). \tag{Edmonds}
\]
We say $G$ satisfies the \textbf{Rainbow Forest Inequality (RFI)} at target $t \in \mathbb{Z}$ if $t \le \obj(A)$ for all $A \subseteq E$, which by (Edmonds) is equivalent to the existence of a total rainbow forest of size $t$. When RFI fails, $G$ is an \textbf{obstruction}: some $A$ with $\obj(A) < t$ certifies that no rainbow forest of size $t$ exists.

The guiding conjecture reads: \emph{for a minimal obstruction to total rainbow forests there is a unique subset $A$ with $\obj(A) < t$, and the failure is strict for no other subset.} Interpreting ``minimal'' via edge deletion, we call $G$ an \textbf{edge-minimal obstruction} if RFI fails for $G$ but holds for every single-edge deletion $G - e$. Our central finding is that no such object exists.

\section{Definitions}

Fix a finite ground set $\alpha$ (thought of as $E(G)$), with complementation relative to $E = \alpha$. Let $r_1, r_2 : 2^\alpha \to \mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{definition}[Objective]
$\obj(A) = r_1(A) + r_2(\comp{A})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rainbow Forest Inequality]
For $t \in \mathbb{Z}$, $\RFI(t) :\equiv \forall A \subseteq E,\ t \le \obj(A)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Monotone rank]
$r$ is \emph{monotone} if $X \subseteq Y \implies r(X) \le r(Y)$. Every matroid rank function is monotone.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Common independent set]
$I \subseteq E$ is a \emph{common independent set} of $r_1, r_2$ if $\forall X \subseteq I,\ r_1(X) = |X|$ and $r_2(X) = |X|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Deletion objective and DeletionRFI]
For an edge $e$, the deletion $G - e$ lives on $E \setminus \{e\}$; its objective at $A \subseteq E \setminus \{e\}$ is $r_1(A) + r_2\big((E \setminus \{e\}) \setminus A\big)$. Define $\mathrm{DeletionRFI}(t,e) :\equiv \forall A \subseteq E \setminus \{e\},\ t \le r_1(A) + r_2\big((E\setminus\{e\})\setminus A\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Obstruction; edge-minimal obstruction]
$G$ is an \emph{obstruction at $t$} if $\neg\RFI(t)$. It is \emph{edge-minimal} if additionally $\mathrm{DeletionRFI}(t,e)$ holds for every $e$.
\end{definition}

\section{The Rainbow Forest Inequality (weak duality)}

\begin{theorem}[Rainbow Forest Inequality]\label{thm:rfi}
Let $r_1, r_2$ be monotone. If $I$ is a common independent set, then for every $A \subseteq E$,
\[
|I| \le \obj(A) = r_1(A) + r_2(\comp{A}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Partition $I$ by $A$: $|I| = |I \cap A| + |I \setminus A|$. Since $I \cap A \subseteq I$, common independence gives $r_1(I \cap A) = |I \cap A|$; with $I \cap A \subseteq A$ and monotonicity, $|I \cap A| \le r_1(A)$. Likewise $r_2(I \setminus A) = |I \setminus A|$ and $I \setminus A \subseteq \comp{A}$ give $|I \setminus A| \le r_2(\comp{A})$. Summing yields $|I| \le r_1(A) + r_2(\comp{A}) = \obj(A)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[RFI from a witness]\label{cor:witness}
If there is a common independent set $I$ with $|I| = t$, then $\RFI(t)$ holds.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
For any $A$, Theorem~\ref{thm:rfi} gives $t = |I| \le \obj(A)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Edge deletion and the collapse of edge-minimality}

\begin{theorem}[Deletion monotonicity]\label{thm:delmono}
Let $r_1, r_2$ be monotone. For every edge $e$ and every $A \subseteq E$,
\[
r_1(A \setminus \{e\}) + r_2\big((E \setminus \{e\}) \setminus (A \setminus \{e\})\big) \le \obj(A).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $A \setminus \{e\} \subseteq A$, monotonicity gives $r_1(A \setminus \{e\}) \le r_1(A)$. Next, if $x \in (E \setminus \{e\}) \setminus (A \setminus \{e\})$ then $x \ne e$, $x \in E$, $x \notin A \setminus \{e\}$; with $x \ne e$ this forces $x \notin A$, i.e. $x \in \comp{A}$. Hence $(E \setminus \{e\}) \setminus (A \setminus \{e\}) \subseteq \comp{A}$ and monotonicity of $r_2$ gives the second bound. Add them.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Deletion preserves obstructions]\label{cor:preserve}
If $\obj(A) < t$ for some $A$, then for every edge $e$, with $A' = A \setminus \{e\}$,
\[
r_1(A') + r_2\big((E\setminus\{e\})\setminus A'\big) \le \obj(A) < t.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[One good deletion forces RFI]\label{thm:onegood}
Let $r_1, r_2$ be monotone. If $\mathrm{DeletionRFI}(t,e)$ holds for some $e$, then $\RFI(t)$ holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $A \subseteq E$. Applying $\mathrm{DeletionRFI}(t,e)$ to $A \setminus \{e\} \subseteq E \setminus \{e\}$ gives $t \le r_1(A\setminus\{e\}) + r_2\big((E\setminus\{e\})\setminus(A\setminus\{e\})\big)$, which is $\le \obj(A)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:delmono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No edge-minimal obstruction]\label{thm:nomin}
For monotone $r_1, r_2$ there is no edge-minimal obstruction at any target $t$ on a nonempty ground set: ``$\neg\RFI(t)$ and $\mathrm{DeletionRFI}(t,e)$ for all $e$'' is contradictory.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\mathrm{DeletionRFI}(t,e)$ holds for every $e$, pick any $e_0$; Theorem~\ref{thm:onegood} gives $\RFI(t)$, contradicting $\neg\RFI(t)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Non-vacuity: an honest obstruction}

Let $E = \{a,b\}$ (two edges) with both matroids free, $r_1(A) = r_2(A) = |A|$. Both are monotone matroid ranks. Then $\obj(A) = |A| + |\comp{A}| = 2$ for every $A$. Taking $t = 3$, the Rainbow Forest Inequality fails for every subset ($\obj(A) = 2 < 3$), so this is a genuine obstruction (the largest total rainbow forest has size $2$). Consistently with Corollary~\ref{cor:preserve}, every deletion has minimum objective $1 < 3$ and inherits the failure. Theorems~\ref{thm:delmono}--\ref{thm:nomin} are therefore not vacuous; here the certificate lattice of Section~6 is the entire Boolean lattice.

\section{What survives: the lattice of certificates}

Fix an obstruction and let $\mathcal{F} = \{A : \obj(A) = \min_B \obj(B)\}$. Since $r_1$ is submodular and $A \mapsto r_2(\comp{A})$ is submodular (complementation reverses the lattice while preserving the submodular inequality), $\obj$ is submodular; hence its minimizers are closed under union and intersection:
\[
A, B \in \mathcal{F} \implies A \cup B,\ A \cap B \in \mathcal{F}.
\]
So $\mathcal{F}$ is a distributive sublattice of $2^E$ with unique least element $A_{\min} = \bigcap \mathcal{F}$ and greatest element $A_{\max} = \bigcup \mathcal{F}$. Naive uniqueness (exactly one failing subset) is false, but is replaced by: \emph{the failing certificates form a distributive sublattice with a unique smallest and largest element, while edge-minimal obstructions do not exist at all.}

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Contraction minimality.} Deletion cannot repair RFI; contraction $G/e$ changes ranks non-monotonically. Is there a contraction-minimal notion supporting a uniqueness statement?
\item \textbf{Sharp uniqueness criterion.} Characterize when $A_{\min} = A_{\max}$: e.g. strict submodularity of a rank, or exchange-graph connectivity.
\item \textbf{Quantitative deletion bound.} Determine the $e$ with $\min\obj_{G-e} = \min\obj_G$; these are the edges in every maximum total rainbow forest.
\item \textbf{Full Edmonds min--max.} Prove the strong direction (existence of a common independent set meeting the minimum).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

We proved the weak-duality Rainbow Forest Inequality and a monotonicity principle showing the Edmonds objective can only decrease under edge deletion. Consequently RFI-failure is closed under deletion, so edge-minimal obstructions do not exist for monotone matroid ranks. The genuine structure of the conjecture lies in the distributive sublattice of failing certificates for a fixed obstruction, with unique smallest and largest elements. The arguments use only monotonicity and submodularity of rank, hence apply to arbitrary matroid pairs.

\end{document}

